    pmh: "Hypertension (diagnosed 1 year ago), Dyslipidaemia (known, not on treatment). Family history: father and two siblings have Type 2 Diabetes.. Also has Dyslipidaemia.",
    medHx: "Amlodipine 5mg daily",
    allergies: "Penicillin (rash)",
    pe: "Obese (BMI 33). Mild acanthosis nigricans on neck. Central obesity (waist circumference 109 cm). Fundoscopy: normal retina (no diabetic retinopathy). Foot exam: normal. BP 138/85.",
    vitals: "HR: 102 bpm, BP: 97/79 mmHg, RR: 23 breaths/min, Temp: 37.5°C, SpO2: 90%",
    labs: "Fasting glucose: 210 mg/dL (11.7 mmol/L). Random glucose: 310 mg/dL (17.2 mmol/L). HbA1c: 9.8%. Fasting lipid panel: LDL 160 mg/dL, HDL 35 mg/dL, Triglycerides 280 mg/dL. Creatinine 0.9 mg/dL, eGFR 78. Urinalysis: 2+ glucose, no ketones, no protein. ALT 55 (mild elevation — possible NAFLD).",
    imaging: "No acute imaging needed.",
    diagnosis: "Newly Diagnosed Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus with Poor Glycaemic Control (HbA1c 9.8%) and Co-morbid Metabolic Syndrome",
    ddx: ["Type 2 Diabetes", "Type 1 Diabetes", "Secondary Diabetes (Cushings, acromegaly)", "Stress Hyperglycaemia", "Pancreatic Insufficiency"],
    goals: "Achieve glycaemic target (HbA1c <7%), preserve beta-cell function, reduce cardiovascular risk, manage weight",
    pharm: "1. First-line therapy: Metformin 500mg daily with evening meal for 1 week, then increase to 500mg BID. Target dose 1g BID or 2g extended-release daily as tolerated. 2. If metformin intolerant or HbA1c remains >7% after 3 months: add SGLT2i (Dapagliflozin 10mg daily or Empagliflozin 10-25mg daily) or DPP4i (Sitagliptin 100mg daily). 3. Statin: Atorvastatin 20mg daily for CV risk reduction. 4. ACEi/ARB: consider Ramipril 2.5mg daily or Losartan 25mg daily for nephroprotection if UACR >30.",
    nonPharm: "Medical nutrition therapy: reduce carbohydrate and sugar intake, portion control, replace refined grains with whole grains. Structured exercise: 150 mins/week moderate activity (brisk walking). Weight loss goal: 5-10% of body weight over 6 months. Diabetes self-management education.",
    carePlan: "Start Metformin and titrate. Start Atorvastatin. Refer to diabetes educator, dietitian, and for diabetes self-management education. Check HbA1c in 3 months. Initiate annual screening: retinal exam, urine albumin, foot exam. Discuss glucose self-monitoring.",
    dtps: "1. New diagnosis of T2DM with HbA1c >9% requiring pharmacotherapy and lifestyle intervention. 2. Cardiovascular risk factors (hypertension, obesity, dyslipidaemia, family history) requiring comprehensive management.",
    monitoring: "HbA1c every 3 months until target, then twice yearly. Fasting glucose and weight at each visit. Annual: lipid panel, serum creatinine + eGFR, urinary albumin/creatinine ratio, dilated eye exam, foot exam. BP at each visit.",
    counselling: "Type 2 Diabetes is a serious but manageable condition. Metformin is the first-line medication — take with meals to minimise stomach upset. Weight loss and dietary changes can dramatically improve blood sugar — some patients even achieve remission. Check your feet daily for cuts or blisters. Join a diabetes support group.",
    followUp: "Diabetes clinic review in 3 months. Repeat HbA1c. Titrate medications. If HbA1c >7% on metformin monotherapy, consider adding Dapagliflozin or a GLP-1 receptor agonist for additional glucose control and weight loss.",
    pearls: "Metformin is first-line for T2DM due to efficacy, safety, low cost, and cardiovascular benefit. It does NOT cause hypoglycaemia when used alone. HbA1c target of <7% is appropriate for most adults (individualise: <8% for elderly or with complications). In Kenya, access to Metformin, Glibenclamide, and Insulin is generally good via KEMSA; access to newer agents (SGLT2i, GLP-1 RA, DPP4i) is limited by cost.",
    references: ["ADA Standards of Care in Diabetes 2024", "IDF Clinical Practice Recommendations for T2DM", "Kenya Clinical Guidelines: Diabetes Mellitus"],
