    medHx: "Insulin Glargine 22 units SC at bedtime, Insulin Aspart 6-8 units with meals (stopped 4 days ago)",
    allergies: "NKDA",
    pe: "Tachycardic, tachypnoeic. Deep sighing respirations. Fruity breath. Dry mucous membranes. Mild suprapubic tenderness. Drowsy but arousable (GCS 14).",
    vitals: "HR: 134 bpm, BP: 75/40 mmHg, RR: 25 breaths/min, Temp: 39.7°C, SpO2: 90%",
    labs: "Glucose 450 mg/dL (25 mmol/L). VBG: pH 7.08, HCO3 7, pCO2 20, BE -16. Serum ketones: 6.8 mmol/L. Na 132, K 5.5, Cl 98, Cr 1.3. HbA1c 13.1%. Urinalysis: 4+ glucose, 4+ ketones, positive leukocyte esterase and nitrites. Urine culture: pending.",
    imaging: "No acute imaging.",
    diagnosis: "Severe Diabetic Ketoacidosis Precipitated by Insulin Omission and Urinary Tract Infection",
    ddx: ["DKA", "Hyperosmolar Hyperglycaemic State", "Acute Pancreatitis", "Sepsis", "Intra-abdominal Infection"],
    goals: "Correct DKA, treat underlying UTI, identify and address barriers to insulin adherence, initiate mental health support",
    pharm: "1. DKA protocol: IV Normal Saline 1L over 1 hour, then 250-500 mL/hour. IV Regular Insulin 0.1 units/kg bolus then 0.1 units/kg/hour infusion. Add K+ when <5.5. Add 5% dextrose when glucose <250 mg/dL. 2. Antibiotics: IV Ceftriaxone 1g daily for UTI (empiric, adjust per culture). 3. Once DKA resolved: restart basal-bolus insulin. Assess for home support. 4. Mental health: Depression screening (PHQ-9). Refer to counselling/psychiatry.",
    nonPharm: "ICU/HDU monitoring. Social work consult for adherence barriers. Diabetes educator consult.",
    carePlan: "Treat DKA per protocol. Treat UTI. After resolution: explore reasons for insulin omission — needle phobia? cost? depression? lack of family support? Simplify insulin regimen if possible (e.g., Insulin Degludec once daily + Aspart). Consider insulin pump if available and suitable. Refer to psychologist/psychiatrist for depression management.",
    dtps: "1. Recurrent DKA from insulin non-adherence requiring urgent medical and psychosocial intervention. 2. Underlying mental health condition (depression) contributing to self-care deficits. 3. Concomitant UTI precipitating metabolic crisis.",
    monitoring: "Hourly glucose. q2-4h VBG, electrolytes. I/O. Temperature, WBC, CRP for infection. Assess mood and suicide risk. Assess home environment and family support.",
    counselling: "Insulin is not optional — it is life-saving. I understand injections are difficult, but missing them leads to DKA, which is dangerous and requires hospitalisation. We need to find a way to make diabetes management work for you. Are you willing to talk to a counsellor about how you are feeling?",
    followUp: "Diabetes clinic in 1 week. Mental health follow-up. Social worker for adherence support. Consider community health worker home visits.",
    pearls: "Recurrent DKA is always a medical and psychosocial emergency. Ask about: insulin access, injection technique, dietary issues, mental health, family support, alcohol/substance use. Every DKA admission is an opportunity to intervene on the root cause. Depression is 2-3x more common in people with diabetes and significantly impacts self-care.",
    references: ["ADA Standards of Care 2024: Psychosocial Care", "ISPAD Guidelines: DKA", "Kenya Clinical Guidelines: Diabetes and Mental Health"],
