    pharm: "1. IV fluids: 0.9% Normal Saline 1L over 1 hour, then 500-1000 mL/hour. Reassess haemodynamics. When BP stable and euvolemic, switch to 0.45% NaCl if corrected Na >150 mmol/L. Total fluid deficit is typically 8-12L. 2. Insulin: low-dose IV Regular Insulin 0.05 units/kg/hour (HHS is less insulin-sensitive than DKA). Start only after adequate fluid resuscitation. Target glucose decrease 50-75 mg/dL/hour. 3. Add 5% Dextrose when glucose <300 mg/dL. 4. Anticoagulation: prophylactic LMWH (Enoxaparin 40mg SC daily) due to high thrombotic risk. 5. Treat precipitating cause: antibiotics for infection.",
    nonPharm: "Monitor urine output hourly (Foley catheter). Cardiac monitoring. Central line if haemodynamically unstable. Nutritional support.",
    carePlan: "Admit to HDU/ICU. Aggressive IV fluids (patients with HHS typically have 8-12L deficit). Slow correction of hyperosmolality (target decrease of 3-5 mOsm/kg/hour). Insulin at lower dose than DKA. Monitor for thromboembolic events. Identify precipitant (infection in this case). Transition to SC insulin when glucose <300 mg/dL and patient eating.",
    dtps: "1. HHS with severe volume depletion and AKI requiring aggressive but cautious fluid resuscitation. 2. High thrombotic risk requiring prophylactic anticoagulation. 3. Precipitating infection causing reduced oral intake and medication omission.",
    monitoring: "Hourly: glucose, vitals, I/O. q4h: Na, K, Cl, BUN, Cr, calculated osmolality. q6h: VBG. Daily: ECG, cardiac enzymes. Monitor for fluid overload. Monitor for thrombosis. Neuro checks for cerebral oedema.",
    counselling: "This condition developed because of severe dehydration caused by very high blood sugar levels. The most important treatment is IV fluids — you need several litres. Insulin is given in lower doses than for DKA. We found an infection that likely triggered this episode. Recovery takes several days.",
    followUp: "Endocrine clinic in 2 weeks after discharge. Review diabetes medications (discontinue sulfonylurea, consider adding SGLT2i or GLP-1 RA if renal function recovers). Ensure sick-day management plan.",
    pearls: "HHS has a higher mortality (5-20%) than DKA (<1%) — largely due to older age and comorbidities. Key difference from DKA: minimal ketosis (enough insulin to suppress lipolysis but not enough to promote glucose utilisation). The fluid deficit in HHS is massive (8-12L vs 3-5L in DKA). Serum osmolality >330 is the hallmark. Cerebral oedema is a risk if glucose is corrected too quickly (decrease no more than 50-75 mg/dL/hour).",
    references: ["ADA Consensus Report: DKA and HHS", "Joint British Diabetes Societies: HHS Guidelines", "Kenya Clinical Guidelines: Hyperglycaemic Emergencies"],
