    carePlan: "Start Levothyroxine 50 mcg daily. Recheck TSH and free T4 in 6 weeks. Titrate by 25 mcg increments until TSH in normal range. Once stable, monitor TSH annually. Advise that symptom improvement takes 2-4 weeks for energy, weight loss may take 2-3 months.",
    dtps: "1. Thyroid hormone deficiency requiring lifelong replacement therapy. 2. Secondary dyslipidaemia that may resolve with thyroid replacement. 3. Risk of overtreatment (iatrogenic hyperthyroidism) if dose too high.",
    monitoring: "TSH and free T4 every 6 weeks until stable, then annually. Monitor for symptoms of over-replacement (palpitations, anxiety, insomnia, weight loss, heat intolerance). Assess for symptom resolution at each visit. Lipid panel in 6 months.",
    counselling: "Levothyroxine is NOT a \"pick-me-up\" or weight loss drug — it replaces the hormone your thyroid cannot produce. Take it on an empty stomach with water, 30-60 min before breakfast. Do not take with coffee, tea, calcium, or iron. It takes 6-8 weeks to see the full effect of a dose change. You will likely need this medication for life.",
    followUp: "Endocrine clinic in 6 weeks for TSH check and dose titration. Once stable, annual TSH monitoring. Annual thyroid exam to assess goitre size (may regress with treatment).",
    pearls: "Levothyroxine has a narrow therapeutic index — consistency in brand, timing, and absorption is critical. Switching brands can alter TSH levels. In young healthy patients, start at full replacement dose (1.6 mcg/kg). In elderly or those with cardiac disease, start low (12.5-25 mcg) and increase slowly. Pregnancy increases levothyroxine requirements by 30-50%. Subclinical hypothyroidism (TSH 4.5-10 with normal T4) is controversial — treat if TPO-positive, symptomatic, or pregnant.",
    references: ["ATA Guidelines: Hypothyroidism", "ETA Guidelines: Subclinical Hypothyroidism", "Kenya Clinical Guidelines: Thyroid Disorders"],
