    imaging: "Spine X-ray: Wedge compression fracture of T10 with ~25% height loss. Vertebral bodies: generalised osteopenia. DXA scan (done after): Lumbar spine T-score -3.2, Femoral neck T-score -2.8.",
    diagnosis: "Osteoporotic Vertebral Compression Fracture (T10) in the Setting of Postmenopausal Osteoporosis and Vitamin D Deficiency",
    ddx: ["Osteoporotic Fracture", "Traumatic Vertebral Fracture", "Malignant Spinal Cord Compression", "Osteomalacia", "Paget Disease of Bone"],
    goals: "Relieve acute back pain, treat underlying osteoporosis to prevent future fractures, correct vitamin D deficiency, reduce fall risk",
    pharm: "1. Acute pain management: Paracetamol 1g QID. Consider short-term weak opioid (Tramadol 50mg PRN) if pain severe. Avoid NSAIDs (renal risk in elderly). 2. Calcium and Vitamin D: Calcium carbonate 1000mg daily + Vitamin D3 2000 IU daily (loading for 8-12 weeks, then maintenance 800-1000 IU/day). 3. Antiresorptive therapy: Start a bisphosphonate — Alendronate 70mg ONCE WEEKLY (take with full glass of water on empty stomach, stay upright for 30 min). Or Zoledronic Acid 5mg IV once yearly (if unable to tolerate oral bisphosphonate). 4. If severe or contraindication to bisphosphonate: consider Denosumab 60mg SC every 6 months or Teriparatide (PTH analogue) 20mcg SC daily. 5. Fall prevention: home safety assessment, balance exercises, gait aid if needed.",
    nonPharm: "Short period of bed rest (<1 week, to avoid deconditioning). Physiotherapy for core strengthening and balance training. Spinal brace for comfort if needed (short-term). Weight-bearing exercise when pain resolves (walking). Dietary: calcium-rich foods (milk, yoghurt, sardines, kale).",
    carePlan: "Treat acute fracture pain. Start Calcium + Vitamin D loading dose. Start weekly Alendronate. Refer to physiotherapy and falls clinic. Assess for secondary causes of osteoporosis (thyroid, Cushings, medications). Re-check 25(OH) Vitamin D in 3 months. Repeat DXA scan in 2 years to assess treatment response.",
    dtps: "1. Established osteoporosis with fragility fracture requiring pharmacotherapy. 2. Vitamin D deficiency contributing to bone loss. 3. High risk of future fractures (hip, spine, wrist) requiring comprehensive bone protection.",
    monitoring: "Adherence to weekly bisphosphonate (alendronate) or yearly IV infusion. Monitor for side effects: GI upset (alendronate), osteonecrosis of the jaw (rare, with dental screening before starting), atypical femur fracture (rare). 25(OH) Vitamin D level in 3 months. DXA in 2 years.",
    counselling: "Osteoporosis is a silent disease — the fracture is the first sign. Falls can be prevented with home modifications (remove rugs, improve lighting, grab bars in bathroom). Take your weekly alendronate correctly to avoid oesophageal irritation. Weight-bearing exercise (walking, dancing) strengthens bones. Smoking cessation and limiting alcohol are essential.",
    followUp: "Falls clinic review. Physiotherapy for balance and gait training. Repeat DXA in 2 years. Dental check before starting bisphosphonate (if needed). Annual review of bone health.",
    pearls: "A fragility fracture (fracture from a fall from standing height or less) is diagnostic of osteoporosis regardless of DXA T-score. All patients with fragility fractures should be treated. Bisphosphonates reduce hip fracture risk by 50% and vertebral fracture risk by 50-70%. After 5 years of alendronate, reassess fracture risk — some patients may benefit from a drug holiday. Denosumab should not be stopped without switching to a bisphosphonate (rapid bone loss and rebound fracture risk).",
    references: ["NOF Guidelines: Osteoporosis Management", "AACE/ACE Guidelines: Postmenopausal Osteoporosis", "Kenya Clinical Guidelines: Metabolic Bone Disease"],
